</form></div></li>
</ul></div><!-- #EndLibraryItem --><h1>How-to: Create Batch file macros</h1=
>=20
<p>A macro allows you to embed blocks of code in a variable. Like calling a=
 <a href=3D"https://ss64.com/nt/call.html">subroutine</a> or <a href=3D"htt=
ps://ss64.com/nt/syntax-functions.html">function</a> this allows reusing th=
e same block of code multiple times, the difference is that by placing the =
code in a variable the performance will be much faster.</p>
<p>This is an advanced technique but can be very useful in scripts that inc=
lude large blocks of code or looping commands where the performance of othe=
r methods is too slow.</p>
<p>A  macro which runs <a href=3D"https://ss64.com/nt/exit.html">exit /b</a=
></p>
<blockquote>
<p class=3D"code">set _ex=3Dexit /b</p>
</blockquote>
<p>You can then exit a subroutine with:</p>
<blockquote>
<p class=3D"code">%_ex%</p>
</blockquote>
<p>A   macro which lists all the .CSV files in a folder:</p>
<blockquote>
<p class=3D"code">Set _macro=3DDir c:\demo\*.csv</p>
</blockquote>
<p>We can now run the Macro like this:</p>
<blockquote>
<p><span class=3D"code">%_macro%</span><br>
</p>
</blockquote>
<p>So far so like the <a href=3D"https://ss64.com/nt/doskey.html">DOSKEY</a=
> command, but to make this more powerful you will want to pass arguments t=
o the macro, in the example above you might want to pass the name of the fo=
lder/file to be listed.</p>
<p>We can use a placeholder character, (in this case #) and then use <a hre=
f=3D"https://ss64.com/nt/syntax-replace.html">variable String Substitution<=
/a> to swap that out at runtime:</p>
<blockquote>
<p class=3D"code">SET _macroDir=3DDir # /s</p>
</blockquote>
<p>We can now run the Macro to get a file listing including subdirectories =
like this:</p>
<blockquote>
<p><span class=3D"code">%_macroDir:#=3Dc:\demo\*.csv%<br>%_macroDir:#=3Dc:\=
work\*.txt%</span><br>
</p>
</blockquote>
Another example which counts numbers:
<blockquote>
<p class=3D"code">SET "_macroCount=3DFOR /L %%G in (1, 1, #) DO (Echo %%G)"=
  </p>
</blockquote>
<p>Calling that macro and passing 3</p>
<blockquote>
<p class=3D"code">%_macroCount:#=3D3%  </p>
<p>Will output:</p>
<p class=3D"code">1<br>
2<br>
3</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Passing multiple arguments to a macro is a little more complex, the  met=
hod discovered by <a href=3D"https://www.dostips.com/forum/viewtopic.php?f=
=3D3&amp;t=3D2518">Jeb</a>, is to place a <span class=3D"code"><a href=3D"h=
ttps://ss64.com/nt/for_l.html">For /L</a></span> command within the macro, =
set to run 2 steps each time the macro is run:</p>
<ul>
<li>In the first step the arguments are stored in a variable (<span class=
=3D"code">argv</span>)</li>
<li>In the second step the main body of the macro runs and can (optionally)=
 read the variable</li>
</ul>
<p>The basic structure of the macro definition:</p>
<pre>Set _macro=3DFor /l %%n in (1 1 2) do if %%n=3D=3D2 (Echo Main MACRO g=
oes here.) else setlocal enableDelayedExpansion ^&amp; Set argv=3D,=20

%_macro% arg1 arg2

 :: The macro will expand to:
 :: for /l %%n in (1 1 2) do if %%n=3D=3D2 (Echo Main MACRO goes here.) els=
e setlocal enableDelayedExpansion &amp; Set argv=3D, arg1 arg2

 :: which is equivalent to:
 ::    setlocal enableDelayedExpansion
 ::    Set argv=3D arg1 arg2
 ::    Echo Main MACRO goes here.</pre>
<h3><a id=3D"e" tabindex=3D"-1"></a>Examples</h3>
<pre>@Echo off
cls
setlocal DisableDelayedExpansion
set LF=3D^


::Above 2 blank lines are required - do not remove
set ^"\n=3D^^^%LF%%LF%^%LF%%LF%^^"

set _macrodemo=3Dfor /L %%n in (1 1 2) do if %%n=3D=3D2 <b>(</b>%\n%
      for /F "tokens=3D1 delims=3D, " %%G in ("!argv!") do (%\n%
         echo _argument1=3D%%G %\n%
         set "_argument1=3D!%%~G!"%\n%
         dir !_argument1!<b>\*.xls</b>%\n%
      ) %\n%
<b>)</b> ELSE setlocal enableDelayedExpansion ^&amp; set argv=3D,

set "_testfolder=3D<b>c:\demo</b>"
%_macrodemo% _testfolder</pre>
<p>In a macro where more than one argument need to be passed, these will be=
come tokens 2,3,4... in the FOR / F command.</p>
<p>A library of macros can be defined by running a single MACROS.CMD batch =
file, allowing the library macros to be used in multiple batch files.</p>
<p>Credits: Ed Dyreen, Jeb and Dave Benham, the original dostips forum thre=
ads are <a href=3D"https://www.dostips.com/forum/viewtopic.php?f=3D3&amp;t=
=3D1827">here</a> (basic concepts) and <a href=3D"https://www.dostips.com/f=
orum/viewtopic.php?f=3D3&amp;t=3D2518">here</a> (macros with parameters).</=
p>
<p><span class=3D"quote"><i>=E2=80=9CMacro dictionary definition: 1. Large =
2. Long 3. Inclusive=E2=80=9D ~ from the Greek 'makros'</i></span></p>
